 expert ratings point Republican, split between Solid/Safe R and Likely R", "Polymarket prices Hamadeh near 87 percent", "Hamadeh holds a large fundraising edge; the assault-story coverage gives the Democrat attention but no polling shows movement"], "rationale": "The district's R+8 lean, Hamadeh's incumbency, and his roughly seven-to-one fundraising advantage over Greene-Placentia anchor the race firmly on the Republican side, and every expert rating and the prediction market agree. With no general-election polling available, a mid-to-high single-digit Republican margin best balances the ratings consensus against market pricing.", "watch": "Sustained national attention to the assault story, or late polling showing it moving West Valley swing voters, could tighten the race.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Abraham Hamadeh (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Arizona's 8th District, a Republican-leaning seat in the Phoenix West Valley, features first-term incumbent Abraham Hamadeh against Democrat Bernadette Greene-Placentia. The district's R+8 lean, Hamadeh's incumbency, and his large fundraising advantage frame the contest as firmly Republican-leaning. Ten expert ratings all point the same way, split between Solid/Safe R and Likely R, and prediction markets price Hamadeh's chances near 87 percent.\n\nNo general-election polling is available, so the outlook rests on fundamentals, ratings, and markets. The campaign's most prominent storyline has been Greene-Placentia's disclosure that she was assaulted while repairing campaign signs, followed by Hamadeh offering a reward in the case; the episode has drawn wide coverage but no clear sign it has shifted the race. Hamadeh remains the strong favorite, with Greene-Placentia needing a substantial partisan shift to contend.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0839, "margin": -8.56}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0